## M12: an unsettled hold becomes spend, without charging anything twice

What does a replica killed with requests in flight cost the ledger while
Redis is up, and does charging an expired hold close it without
double-charging the holds the degraded path already leaves to expire?
Commands: `make chaos-orphan RUN=before|after`, the flap schedule re-run
(`SCHEDULE=flap OUT=eval/results/m12-orphan/flap-rerun chaos/run.sh`),
and `make sim-budget SEEDS=100000`; raw files in
`eval/results/m12-orphan/` and `eval/results/m12-budget-sim/`.

**The fix.** An expired hold is charged as `spent` at its held amount
(the input estimate plus `max_tokens`, the request's worst case) by the
next script that touches the key, and its id is kept with the amount in
a tombstone hash that lives as long as the window's keys. A settle that
finds the tombstone corrects `spent` by actual − held instead of adding
the cost again. A replica that settles a Redis hold while Redis is
unreachable keeps that cost in pending under the hold's id, so the flush
after the outage settles the hold (or corrects its tombstone) instead of
charging beside it. `memory.go` does the same under the ledger contract
tests; `TestIntegrationExpiry` runs expiry and a late settle on a real
Redis, and `TestDegradingParkedSettleOutlivesTwoHoldTTLs` flushes a
grace-time settle after an outage of two and a half hold TTLs and
charges it once (with the ledger fix alone and no id in pending, that
test charges 80 + 30 for a request that cost 30).

Not measured yet. Run `make chaos-orphan RUN=before`, `make chaos-orphan
RUN=after` and `make sim-budget SEEDS=100000`, then `make results`.

## Unmodified SDKs through the gateway

Does a stock client stream a reply through the gateway, whichever wire
format it speaks and whichever backend serves it? Command: `make sdk-smoke`
(`eval/sdk/smoke.sh`), raw files in `eval/results/sdk-smoke/`. Each case
streams one short prompt and then makes the same request without streaming;
it passes when the SDK's own parser delivered the text in more than one
chunk with a stop reason and usage, and the non-streamed call returned text.
`-via-messages` is the same Ollama model reached through the gateway's
Anthropic provider and Ollama's `/v1/messages`, so the four rows per model
cover every client × backend translation. TTFT is one sample, measured in
the client, and says nothing about the gateway's overhead (Q1 does).

Not measured yet. Start Ollama with `qwen2.5:0.5b`, run `make sdk-smoke`, then `make results`.

## Known problems

Measured weaknesses and cases the runs could not reach, each with what was
observed.

- Budget reconciliation under a flapping store: not measured yet
  (`make chaos-flap`).
- **About two seconds degraded after Redis comes back.** go-redis's
  dial backoff and the gateway's one-second probe run one after the
  other, so a replica keeps using its local limits, grace and no cache for
  about two seconds after Redis is ready (M9, every mode). The cache is
  the visible cost: hits lost, not wrong answers.
- **`everysec` can lose the last second of charges to a machine crash.**
  A killed Redis process loses nothing it acknowledged (the kernel holds
  the written bytes), which is the case M9's container kill tested; a
  crash of the machine or kernel in the middle of a key's spending
  forgets up to about a second of its writes, and no run here produces
  one. With no append-only file (`REDIS_PERSIST=none`, Q6) a restart
  forgets every budget.
- **A first-token timeout cannot see a stall after the first token.**
  A provider that sends its first token and then stops holds the request
  until the provider's whole-call `timeout` (10 minutes by default) or the
  client gives up, and the breaker never hears of it. Non-streamed
  requests are not covered either: their reply arrives whole, so only the
  provider's `timeout` bounds them. Neither case is measured.
- **A `LOADING` Redis was not reached under load.** M9's append-only file
  loaded in tens of milliseconds and no replica called during it; the integration
  test shows a `LOADING` reply degrades like an unreachable store and
  recovers, but no chaos run has a Redis that loads for seconds.

